      "chapterTitle": "I. The Yashica FX-3 on the Cedar Verandah",
      "heading": "I. The Yashica FX-3 on the Cedar Verandah",
      "body": "The camera had belonged to their uncle Tarun, who taught physics at St. Joseph's College in Darjeeling and treated every exposure as an experiment in optical refraction.\n\nIt was a mechanical Yashica FX-3, manufactured in Tokyo in 1982, with a manual fifty-millimeter lens, a black vinyl body that smelled of silicone grease and cold mountain air, and a shutter mechanism that tripped with a sharp, dry, metallic click that sounded like a dry twig snapping beneath a boot. Tarun-kaku did not use flashbulbs. He believed that artificial illumination destroyed the integrity of natural shadows; he insisted that human beings should be photographed only in the soft, diffused silver light that filtered through the Himalayan mist between eleven in the morning and two in the afternoon.\n\nOn Sunday, June 12, 1988, he had lined the six cousins up on the cedar verandah steps of Pine View Cottage in Kurseong.\n\nThe cottage stood at an altitude of four thousand eight hundred and sixty-four feet, perched on a steep spur of grey gneiss rock overlooking the deep, emerald trough of the Balason river valley. It had a green corrugated tin roof that amplified every drop of monsoon rain into a drumbeat, deep eaves trimmed with carved wooden bargeboards shaped like ivy leaves, and a wide verandah of unpainted cedar planks that had weathered over sixty monsoons to the pale, silvery grey of driftwood.\n\n\"Stand still,\" Tarun-kaku had shouted from behind the tripod, his head hidden beneath a black focusing cloth. \"Dipak, stop pulling your sister's plait. Malati, look at the lens, not at the valley. Subhash, tuck your shirt in.\"\n\nThey were six children between the ages of eight and twenty-two:\n\nOn the top step stood Dipak, twenty-two, the eldest, already carrying the heavy, confident posture of a young man who had completed his bachelor's degree in agricultural science and had been selected as an assistant manager for the Makaibari tea estate. He was wearing a dark brown woollen pullover with leather elbow patches, his arms folded across his chest, his jaw set with that early, stubborn pride that his family mistook for leadership.\n\nBeside him stood his younger brother, Subhash, eighteen, leaning against the wooden post with his hands shoved deep into the pockets of his flared corduroy trousers, laughing at an private joke.\n\nOn the step below sat Malati, then twenty, wearing a handloom cotton sari of pale mustard yellow, her knees drawn up to her chin, her reading glasses pushed up into her thick black hair, holding a battered paperback edition of Toru Dutt's poetry in her lap.\n\nNext to Malati was her sister Rupa, sixteen, and their two youngest maternal cousins: Tapan, ten, whose right sneaker was torn at the toe and tied with a piece of hairy jute twine because he had kicked a stone down the cart road that morning; and little Jhumpa, eight, holding a half-eaten green plum in her left hand, her chin smeared with yellow salt and red chili powder.\n\nBehind them, the Himalayan mist was rolling up from the valley in slow, white, horizontal plumes that smelled of damp pine needles, lichen, and burning charcoal from the village kitchens below.\n\n*Click-whirrr.*\n\nThe shutter tripped at one-one-hundred-and-twenty-fifth of a second, at aperture f/5.6 on Ilford FP4 black-and-white film.\n\nFor thirty-six years, that single fractional second of exposure had remained frozen in silver halide: six children, six sets of teeth, six pairs of clear, dark eyes looking forward into an open future where none of them had yet lost a parent, none of them had argued over an acre of terraced plum orchard, and none of them had spent twenty-four years walking past each other on the streets of Siliguri without turning their heads.",
