      "chapterTitle": "I-B. The Curfews of 1988 and the Sanctuary of the Ridge",
      "heading": "I-B. The Curfews of 1988 and the Sanctuary of the Ridge",
      "body": "To understand why that photograph in June 1988 was precious, one had to remember what was happening outside the wooden gates of Pine View Cottage.\n\nThe late 1980s in the Darjeeling hills were a time of fire, road blockades, and political fury. The Gorkhaland agitation had shut down the schools, the tea factories, and the narrow-gauge railway for months at a time. The Hill Cart Road was frequently blocked by felled cedar trunks and burning tires; paramilitary patrols in olive-green trucks rumbled along the mountain curves; and food supplies in the town bazaars were rationed under emergency district magistrate orders.\n\nYet on the ridge above Kurseong, Pine View Cottage had remained an enchanted island.\n\nThe six cousins had been stranded there for seven weeks because the state transport buses were suspended. While the plains below in Siliguri sweltered under forty-degree heat and general strikes, the children lived in an autonomous republic of board games, reading, and shared labor.\n\nEvery morning, Dipak would lead them into the woods behind Dow Hill to gather fallen pine cones and dead rhododendron branches for the kitchen hearth.\n\nHe carried a curved Nepali billhook (*khukuri*) in a worn wooden sheath on his belt, showing ten-year-old Tapan how to tell the difference between seasoned oak that would burn clean and white without smoke, and green pine that would spark and soot the chimney. Subhash carried the woven bamboo basket (*doko*) on his back with the braided head-strap across his forehead, while Malati and Rupa picked wild blackberries along the railway embankments, their fingers stained purple with juice.\n\nIn the evenings, when the mountain mist pressed against the window panes like cold fleece, they would huddle around the cast-iron fireplace in the drawing room.\n\nTheir grandmother, an indomitable woman of seventy-four named Sarojbashini who had lived through the Bengal famine of 1943, would roast ears of sweet mountain corn on the red embers, rubbing the charred kernels with lime juice and black rock salt. Uncle Tarun would tune his Philips shortwave radio to the BBC World Service, listening through the static to news of the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan and the Seoul Olympics, while the children played carrom on an ancient wooden board whose corner pockets were made of hand-knitted twine.\n\nThere was no television. There was no telephone.\n\nThe cottage was their entire universe. In that enclosed, threatened warmth, the bond between the six cousins had forged into something absolute—an unspoken pact that whatever storm raged in the valleys below, the people inside Pine View Cottage would always hold the door shut against the dark.\n\nWhen Tarun-kaku had assembled them on the verandah on June 12 for the photograph, it had not been a casual holiday snapshot.\n\nIt was a certificate of survival. It was a visual document asserting that in the middle of a burning province, six young people had stood together on cedar planks, laughing into the mountain wind, completely untouched by the divisions of the adult world.",
