      "id": "sec-ph-3",
      "type": "text-image-right",
      "heading": "II. The Twenty-Four Years of Silence",
      "body": "The fracture had not begun with hatred. It had begun with seven brass survey markers driven into the red clay of the hillside in May 2000.\n\nPine View Cottage had belonged to their maternal grandfather, Rai Bahadur Nilmoni Banerjee, who had served as an executive engineer in the Public Works Department under the British Raj. When he died intestate in 1964, the property—the two-storey cottage and three acres of terraced plum and orange orchard sloping down toward the railway line—had passed jointly to his two daughters: Malati's mother, Snehalata, and Dipak's mother, Kalyani.\n\nFor thirty-six years, the families had shared the house with the easy, chaotic generosity of traditional joint households.\n\nSnehalata's family occupied the ground floor during the summer vacations from Siliguri; Kalyani's family lived upstairs, where Dipak managed the garden after his shifts at the tea factory. They shared the kitchen; they shared the stone water cistern fed by the mountain spring; they shared the produce of the forty-two plum trees, sending baskets of bottled jam to relatives in Calcutta every July.\n\nThen, in the spring of 2000, Dipak had decided to build a concrete garage and outhouse on the eastern terrace to accommodate an imported four-wheel-drive Mahindra pickup he had purchased for his tea-brokerage business.\n\nMalati's father, a quiet, stubborn high-school headmaster from Siliguri named Dhirendranath Ghosh, had objected.\n\n\"The eastern terrace,\" Dhirendranath had said, standing on the grass with his arms folded beneath his shawl, \"is where my wife's share of the orchard begins. If you pour a concrete slab over the spring line, the two plum trees below will wither. The land must be partitioned by an official surveyor before a single bag of cement is mixed.\"\n\nDipak had taken that objection as a mortal insult to his honor.\n\n\"Dhiren-babu,\" Dipak had shouted, his face turning dark with rage, \"I have maintained this roof for twenty years while you lived in the plains! I have repaired the retaining wall after every landslide! You come up for twenty days in June and want to tell me where I can park my vehicle?\"\n\nA land partition in the sub-Himalayan hills is not a legal proceeding; it is a prolonged, agonizing civil war conducted through revenue court notices, village arbitration meetings, and barbed-wire fences strung across terraced gardens.\n\nA government surveyor named Pradhan had arrived from Darjeeling with a brass theodolite and a thirty-meter steel measuring tape. He had driven seven brass markers into the soil, dividing the orchard into two narrow, absurd, unusable strips of mountain slope: Plot A-1 and Plot A-2.\n\nThe two plum trees had been felled to make way for a corrugated tin fence.\n\nFrom that afternoon in May 2000, Dipak Banerjee had stopped speaking to Malati's father.\n\nWhen Dhirendranath died of a stroke in 2012, Dipak had not attended the funeral in Siliguri. When Snehalata died four years later, Dipak had sent a formal envelope containing five hundred rupees for the *shraddha* ceremony, delivered by a messenger from the tea garden. Malati's brother had returned the envelope unopened.\n\nThe silence had hardened into granite.\n\nThe cousins who had stood with their arms interlocked in 1988 became strangers. Subhash had emigrated to Canada and worked in logistics in Mississauga; Jhumpa had married an army officer and lived in military cantonments across Punjab; Tapan had died of a motorcycle accident near Sevoke in 2016.\n\nOnly Malati and Dipak remained in the district—forty miles apart, separated by eighteen hundred meters of vertical elevation and twenty-four years of frozen protocol.",
