      "id": "sec-ph-3c",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II-C. The Winter in Mississauga",
      "heading": "II-C. The Winter in Mississauga",
      "body": "Seven thousand miles away, in a split-level brick house on a suburban crescent in Mississauga, Ontario, Subhash Banerjee had spent twenty-five years living with the phantom of Pine View Cottage.\n\nSubhash had emigrated in 1999, just eight months before the survey markers were driven into the orchard clay.\n\nHe had become a senior operations supervisor for a logistics warehouse near Pearson International Airport, managing eight hundred thousand square feet of temperature-controlled freight racking where forklifts moved with silent, laser-guided precision beneath fluorescent banks. He had a Canadian passport; he drove a silver Honda CR-V with winter snow tires; his two daughters, Ananya and Maya, spoke English with the flat, nasal vowels of southern Ontario and played ice hockey on community rinks in Brampton.\n\nYet on January evenings, when the Canadian blizzards howling off Lake Ontario piled five feet of grey snow against his garage doors, Subhash would sit alone in his basement recreation room with an electric space heater glowing orange between his ankles.\n\nHe would open his laptop and look at the satellite view of Kurseong on Google Earth.\n\nHe would zoom in through the clouds until he could see the green corrugated roof of Pine View Cottage, the dark smudge of the cedar woods, and the thin, pale line of the Hill Cart Road snaking along the contour of the ridge.\n\nHe knew about the partition suit. His father had sent him airmail packets containing photocopies of the plaint, demanding that Subhash contribute two thousand Canadian dollars toward the lawyer's fees in the Siliguri district court.\n\nSubhash had sent the money once. Then he had stopped.\n\n\"Baba,\" Subhash had written from Toronto in the winter of 2003, \"I am clearing snow at six in the morning in twenty-below weather to pay a thirty-year mortgage on a house that has vinyl siding and drywall. I will not send money to hire lawyers to fight my own cousins over three hundred yards of mountain rock. If you and Uncle Dhiren want to destroy the cottage, destroy it. But do not ask me to buy the sledgehammers.\"\n\nHis father had not replied for fourteen months.\n\nWhen Subhash's elder daughter turned sixteen in 2018, she had asked him for an old family photograph for a heritage project at her high school in Oakville.\n\nSubhash had searched his boxes in the attic and found a copy of the Yashica FX-3 print that Uncle Tarun had mailed to him in 1989.\n\nHis daughter had looked at the six cousins in their cotton clothes, their windblown hair, their bare knees and canvas shoes.\n\n\"Dad,\" she had asked, touching the faded grey surface with her fingernail, \"who are all these people? Why have I never met any of them?\"\n\nSubhash had looked at his daughter's clean, innocent face.\n\nHe had tried to explain the British Raj; he had tried to explain the joint property laws of Bengal; he had tried to explain the pride of tea garden managers and high school headmasters. But every explanation he attempted sounded hollow, ridiculous, profoundly shameful when translated into the clear, rational air of an Ontario kitchen.\n\nHow do you tell a Canadian child that she has three aunts and five cousins she will never know because two old men argued about whether a Mahindra pickup could be parked near a plum tree?\n\nHe had closed the photograph and put it back into the drawer.\n\n\"They were my cousins,\" Subhash had said quietly. \"We were very close once. And then we forgot how to talk to each other.\"",
