      "id": "sec-ph-6",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "III. The Hospital Notice from Siliguri",
      "heading": "III. The Hospital Notice from Siliguri",
      "body": "On Thursday, October 10, 2024, Malati was marking essay scripts in the staff room of the Siliguri Girls' High School.\n\nThe second-term examinations had just concluded; forty-two notebooks covered in blue marble paper were stacked on her desk, and the fan overhead was blowing the smell of damp chalk and warm ink across the wooden table.\n\nHer mobile phone vibrated on the desk.\n\nIt was a call from her maternal cousin Bipasha, who lived in Jalpaiguri.\n\n\"Malati-di,\" Bipasha said, her voice hurried, hushed. \"Did you hear about Dipak-da?\"\n\nMalati paused with her red ballpoint pen poised over a student's paragraph on Rabindranath's *Postmaster*.\n\n\"No,\" Malati said, keeping her tone completely flat. \"I haven't spoken to Dipak-da since Ma died.\"\n\n\"He was brought down to the North Bengal Medical College yesterday evening,\" Bipasha said. \"A mild cerebral stroke. His speech is slurred on the left side, and his blood pressure was two hundred and twenty over one hundred and ten. The doctors have stabilized him, but his daughter Suparna says he refused to stay in the hospital. He signed his own discharge papers this morning and hired a private ambulance to take him back up to Pine View Cottage. He said if he is going to die, he will die on his own mountain.\"\n\nMalati set the red pen down on the desk blotter.\n\nThe nib had left a tiny circular dot of crimson ink on the student's paper.\n\n\"Who is with him?\" Malati asked.\n\n\"Nobody,\" Bipasha sighed. \"Suparna lives in Bangalore with her software engineer husband; she is flying in on Sunday. His wife Manju died three years ago during COVID. There is only an old Nepali domestic help named Dil Bahadur who brings him firewood and cooks his soup. The man is completely alone in that two-storey house.\"\n\nMalati hung up.\n\nShe sat in the empty staff room for twenty minutes while the afternoon sun shifted across the yellow distempered walls. Through the open window, the children in the primary section were singing the national anthem on the gravel assembly ground, their clear, shrill voices rising through the dust like sparrows.\n\nShe went home to her rented ground-floor flat in Hakimpara.\n\nShe went to the steel Godrej almirah in her bedroom, unlocked the middle shelf beneath her silk saris, and took out the red clothbound album.\n\nShe opened it on the dining table.\n\nPage twelve: June 12, 1988. Six cousins on the verandah steps.\n\nDipak at twenty-two, with his arms folded and his brown woollen sweater; Tapan with his torn sneaker tied with twine; herself holding Toru Dutt's poems.\n\nShe looked at Dipak's face in the photograph. It was the face of a boy who had spent three hours in the forest behind Pine View Cottage in 1985 building her a wooden swing from wild cane ropes and a plank of split cedar, testing it with his own weight first to make sure she would not fall.\n\nShe called her sister Rupa in Calcutta.\n\n\"Rupa,\" Malati said. \"I am going up to Kurseong tomorrow morning.\"\n\nRupa was silent on the phone for four seconds. \"Are you out of your mind, Didi? Dipak-da did not even attend Baba's funeral. He has spent twenty-four years calling our family thieves in the tea auctions. If you knock on that door, he will close the shutters in your face.\"\n\n\"Let him close them,\" Malati said quietly. \"At least he will have to look at me before he shuts them.\"",
