      "heading": "VII-C. The Descent into the Plains",
      "body": "The four-fifteen bus from Kurseong motor stand was nearly empty as it began its descent along Hill Cart Road.\n\nMalati sat by the window on the valley side. On her lap, wrapped in the handloom tea towel, was the red clothbound album containing the photograph from 1988 and the hand-drawn deed signed by Dipak and herself.\n\nThe sun had dropped behind the western ridges of Nepal, casting long, purple shadows across the deep ravines of the Balason river.\n\nAs the bus wound its way through Tindharia and Mahanadi, the mountain air began to warm, taking on the rich, humid scent of the plains: river silt, wet bamboo groves, and burning mustard husks from the village brick kilns. Below the curves of the road, the lights of Siliguri and the north Bengal plains were spreading out in an immense, glittering sea of yellow and white sparks that stretched all the way to the border of Bangladesh.\n\nMalati took her phone from her bag.\n\nThe signal bars flickered, then held steady: three bars of 4G network as the bus cleared the hills above Sukna.\n\nShe dialed her sister Rupa in Calcutta.\n\nThe phone rang twice. Rupa picked up immediately, her voice tense, sharp with anxiety:\n\n\"Didi? Where are you? Are you safe? Did you reach the cottage?\"\n\nMalati looked out at the lights of the plains rushing beneath the bus window.\n\nA tear, warm, sweet, completely free of bitterness, rolled down the crease of her cheek and fell onto the red book-cloth of the album.\n\n\"I am on the bus coming down, Rupa,\" Malati said. Her voice was light, clear, carrying that deep, musical cadence that had belonged to their mother Snehalata thirty years ago. \"I had tea with Dipak-da. In the blue enamel kettle.\"\n\nThere was a long, breathless silence on the other end of the line.\n\nShe could hear the traffic on the Gariahat flyover behind Rupa's balcony; she could hear the clink of a spoon against a porcelain cup.\n\n\"In the blue kettle?\" Rupa whispered, her voice breaking into a quiet sob. \"He... he made tea in the blue kettle?\"\n\n\"He made tea in the blue kettle,\" Malati smiled. \"And Rupa... pack your bags for next summer. We are going up to Pine View Cottage for the plum harvest. Dipak-da is taking down the fence.\"\n\nThe bus crossed the level crossing at Sukna, the engine roaring as it accelerated onto the wide, straight asphalt highway toward Siliguri.\n\nMalati leaned her forehead against the glass.\n\nThe mountains were behind her now—a massive, dark blue wall rising into the starry Himalayan night, ancient, silent, forgiving, holding eighty years of family life in the hollow of its ridges like an old cedar bowl that had never truly broken.",
